# THE JIT SUBSTRATE, reached through `req` rather than through the bare name.
#
# `jitm = jit` bound the HARDCODED BUILTIN module the compiler synthesises in
# `src/jit.zig` (`lua_jit_init`), which holds exactly
# on/off/flush/status/version/version_num/opt/os/arch — no `alloc`, no `w32`,
# no `seal`, no `call2`, no `sym`, no `addr`. Every one of the six entry points
# the ~1,700-line ARM64 emitter below calls was therefore nil, `jit_substrate_gap()`
# named `jit.alloc`, and the emitter has never executed once. The bare name
# SHADOWS `lib/jit.id`, which implements the whole substrate in Idol over
# mmap/mprotect/dlsym; `req "jit"` reaches that file instead.
#
# Keep this spelled as a `req`. Reverting it to the bare name is not a cosmetic
# change: it silently removes the JIT from the build and the only symptom is a
# benchmark getting slower.
jitm = req "jit"
# The preview1 roster, numbering and errno policy, shared with
# `src/wasm/wasi.id`. Scalars and strings only — see the header of that file
# for why the two hosts cannot be merged into one.
wabi = req "src.wasm.wasi_abi"

# the engine — WASM runtime in Duo. Pure Duo: no @c.emit, no @c.include, no C.
#
# Execution strategy is chosen at run time and is CROSS-ARCHITECTURE
# CONSISTENT: every path computes the same result on every host.
#
#   arm64  -> JIT to native ARM64 (jit mmap/seal/call)
#   other  -> the interpreter, which is architecture-independent
#
# The JIT also falls back to the interpreter whenever it meets an opcode it
# cannot yet emit, so adding architectures or opcodes never changes observable
# behaviour # only speed. `--interp` forces the fallback path, which is how
# the two are checked against each other (cross-architecture parity).

# the engine WASM interpreter — PURE DUO, zero @c.emit/@c.include.
#
# EVERY OPCODE NUMBER IN THIS FILE IS PROJECTED, NOT TYPED. The four
# `-- derived(wasm.opcodes.*)` regions below are written by
# `duo run tools/opcodes.id` from the descriptor table in that file, and
# `DUO_WASM_DERIVE=1 duo run tools/opcodes.id` exits non-zero if they have
# drifted. Do not edit inside a derived region; edit the descriptor and
# re-project.
#
# tools/opcodes.id is in turn answerable to duo's canonical table
# (src/wasm_semantic_gen.zig -> `duo wasm-tables emit` ->
# lib/wasm/ward_mvp_opcodes.id): it re-parses that file and refuses to
# project on any disagreement over the 63 opcodes it holds. the engine needs 170,
# which is why the extension table lives here rather than there.
#
# The constants are inlined rather than required because requiring a std
# module currently fails to compile (BUG C: export table references DCE'd
# symbols). The scripts/gen_interp.py that used to re-emit them was deleted
# 2026-08-07: it read a /tmp/run_body.bin that no longer existed and matched
# the table with `^M\.(OP_\w+)`, a shape the table had already lost.
#
# Codegen constraints (filed 2026-08-05, src/codegen.zig locked):
#   BUG A  a branch may not end on a void call -> explicit non-void tail.
#   BUG B  CLOSED: exact pointer descriptors now cross relation parameters and
#          results; `native_only/pointer_transport.id` is the executable proof.
#   BUG C  cross-home `req` applications do not yet lower from one semantic
#          graph; mem.* remains intercepted by name until that path exists.
#   BUG D  named consts are illegal as `match` patterns -> if/elseif, which
#          compares properly and lets the descriptor names be used directly.
#   BUG E  mem.alloc local + while + `match` is a compiler panic -> if/elseif.
#   BUG G  variable shift >= 32 is 32-bit -> no LEB sign-extension (masking
#          the raw accumulated bits to 32 already yields the correct i32).
#
# Shape: ONE hoisted LEB128 decode (was 6 copies), binops share operand
# fetch/stack adjust (was 15 near-identical blocks), hot opcodes tested first.

# derived(wasm.opcodes.constants) begin # regenerate: duo run tools/opcodes.id
